=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** No subsystem, daemon, interface, or policy is misconfigured or faulty in the current lab state.

**Mechanism:** The user on pc1 reports that 200.1.1.2 (pc2) has been unreachable "since this morning." A complete diagnostic sweep of the simple_bgp lab finds no evidence to support this claim. Live end-to-end ICMP from pc1 to pc2 returns 0% packet loss in both directions (Phase 1, `get_reachability`). The L2 fabric is clean: no duplicate MACs, no down or missing interfaces (`l2_snapshot`). The addressing plane is healthy: both PCs have IPs and default routes, no ARP poisoning, no ACL drop rules (`infra_sweep`). The BGP control plane is fully operational: both router1 (ASN 1, advertising 195.11.14.0/24) and router2 (ASN 2, advertising 200.1.1.0/24) show their single BGP peer in `Established` state, with no blackhole (null-route) leaks and all FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra, bgpd) running (`bgp_snapshot`). There is no traffic shaping on any interface (`tc_snapshot`), and no resource pressure or stress tools on any host (`pressure_sweep`). This topology carries no application-layer services (no web server, no DNS), so there is no service endpoint that could be down.

**Localization:** No device is implicated.

**Root cause:** The reported outage has no current or historical trace in the live lab — the network is fully functional end-to-end.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the user is accessing pc2 via a hostname rather than its IP, they should verify their local name resolution; the routing fabric itself is working correctly.
=== END ===